draft": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/draft", "access_links": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/access/links", "access_grants": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/access/grants", "access_users": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/access/users", "access_request": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/access/request", "access": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/access", "communities": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/communities", "communities-suggestions": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/communities-suggestions", "request_deletion": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/request-deletion", "file_modification": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/file-modification", "quota_increase": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/quota-increase", "requests": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/requests"}, "updated": "2026-02-20T06:06:48.363525+00:00", "recid": "18708049", "revision": 3, "files": [{"id": "807befaa-2e30-4c2a-81c8-b7123c873909", "key": "20260219_v5_The_Oscillation_Principle.pdf", "size": 262465, "checksum": "md5:1ab26fff0e0447b4dfa3742c057d95c2", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/18708049/files/20260219_v5_The_Oscillation_Principle.pdf/content"}}], "swh": null, "owners": [{"id": "1348185"}], "status": "published", "stats": {"downloads": 521, "unique_downloads": 427, "views": 590, "unique_views": 532, "version_downloads": 118, "version_unique_downloads": 107, "version_unique_views": 133, "version_views": 137}, "state": "done", "submitted": true}, {"created": "2025-08-12T00:39:08.324605+00:00", "modified": "2025-12-02T23:01:55.153169+00:00", "id": 16809372, "conceptrecid": "16139573", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.16809372", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.16139573", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16809372", "metadata": {"title": "The General Theory of Entanglement Compression - Explained", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.16809372", "publication_date": "2025-08-11", "description": "<div>\n<p><strong>Title:</strong> <em>The General Theory of Entanglement Compression &ndash; Explained</em><br><strong>Author:</strong> William Andrew Lawrence<br><strong>Date:</strong> August 11, 2025</p>\n<p>This paper presents the updated conceptual summary of <strong>Entanglement Compression Theory (ECT)</strong> &mdash; a unified framework that reinterprets quantum mechanics, relativity, and cosmology through the geometry of informational compression. It replaces traditional assumptions about space, time, and energy with a single generative principle: the <strong>Primordial Wave Equation (PWE)</strong>, which defines how structure emerges from non-dimensional space.</p>\n<p>ECT reframes constants such as <em>c</em>, <em>\u0127</em>, <em>G</em>, and <em>&alpha;</em> as <strong>compression echoes</strong> &mdash; stable ratios that arise from the geometry of entanglement rather than being inserted by hand. It interprets the four fundamental forces as coherence modes within a single compression field, providing geometric unification without invoking new particles or symmetry breaking.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Lawrence Universal Wave Function (LUWF)</strong> serves as the global map of entanglement structure, while the <strong>Lawrence Amplitude-Functional Form (LaFF)</strong> defines the local behavior of amplitude under compression. Together, they describe how space and time emerge from relational updates, not as fixed coordinates but as outcomes of compression gradients.</p>\n<p><strong>Key concepts include:</strong><br>&ndash; Time as a compression gradient, not a fundamental dimension<br>&ndash; Probability as compression loss, not intrinsic randomness<br>&ndash; Collapse as resolution, not discontinuity<br>&ndash; Mass and energy as entanglement weight<br>&ndash; Light as bandwidth, not velocity<br>&ndash; Dark matter and dark energy as curvature artifacts, not exotic substances<br>&ndash; Cycle reversion and the geometry of renewal</p>\n<p>The paper also outlines measurable predictions, including small drift in physical constants, reinterpretations of gravitational lensing, and biological coordination phenomena consistent with compression gradients. It concludes by reframing cosmic evolution not as a linear expansion toward heat death but as a <strong>cyclical braid</strong> governed by coherence and decompression.</p>\n<p>This summary serves as the <strong>conceptual gateway</strong> to the formal ECT canon. It precedes and complements the later mathematical works (<em>Mathematical Foundations of Derived Probability and ECT</em>, <em>The Oscillation Principle</em>, and <em>Unified Derivation of Probability, Curvature, and Compression Geometry</em>).</p>\n<p>For a visual demonstration of deterministic structure emergence under the Primordial Wave Equation (PWE) and its prediction of dark-matter-like halos, see the Compression Theory Institute site: <a href=\"https://compressiontheoryinstitute.org/\">https://compressiontheoryinstitute.org</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Supplement to:</strong> <em>The General Theory of Entanglement Compression</em> -&nbsp; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15786696\" target=\"_new\" rel=\"noopener\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15786696</a></p>\n</div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Lawrence, William Andrew", "affiliation": "Compression Theory Institute", "orcid": "0009-0003-8018-4013"}], "keywords": ["(EuroSciVoc) Quantum physics", "(EuroSciVoc) Theoretical physics", "(EuroSciVoc) Physical cosmology", "(EuroSciVoc) Quantum field theory", "(EuroSciVoc) Mathematical models", "(EuroSciVoc) Fundamental interactions (physics)", "(EuroSciVoc) Physics", "(EuroSciVoc) Physics education", "(EuroSciVoc) Science communication", "(EuroSciVoc) Scientific dissemination"], "references": ["Collett, T. et al. Detection of an ultramassive black hole via gravitational lensing and stellar kinematics in the Cosmic Horseshoe galaxy. Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Socie