, "metadata": {"title": "Logical necessity of Quantum Mechanics", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.8154227", "publication_date": "2023-06-15", "description": "<p>From classical mechanics, in particular the motion in a straight line, together set theory and ordinal number theory, we prove a not-classical behaviour, a discontinuous motion and emission. Now we have obtained that the not-classical behaviour is essentially due to \u2204n(&omega; = S(n)). But incredibly this could have been discovered about 140 years ago, before Planck theory. So quantum behaviour looks very natural and classical mechanics has to be rejected. The classical limit h = 0 cannot exist. The most important theories describe Quantum behavior. It is the first time that classical mechanics is theoretically refuted in favor of quantum mechanics.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Cadeddu, Enrico Pier Giorgio", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0007-1648-327X"}], "keywords": ["Quantum mechanics", "Classical mechanics", "Discontinuous motion", "Zeno paradoxes", "Discontinuous emission", "Cantor", "Dedekind", "Planck Schr\u00f6dinger Heisenberg", "Uncertainty principle", "First transfinite ordinal number \u03c9", "Successor", "Logical necessity"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.21710977", "relation": "isContinuedBy", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}], "language": "eng", "resource_type": {"title": "Journal article", "type": "publication", "subtype": "article"}, "journal": {"issue": "2", "pages": "1-2", "title": "Journal of Modern and Applied Physics", "volume": "6"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-nc-4.0"}, "communities": [{"id": "historyofscience"}], "relations": {"version": [{"index": 4, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "7837324"}}]}}, "title": "Logical necessity of Quantum Mechanics", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/8154227", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/8154227", "preview_html": "https://